(a) **Primary productivity** is the amount of biomass or organic matter produced per unit area over a time period by plants during photosynthesis.
- It is expressed in terms of weight, \(\text{g m}^{-2}\), or energy, \(\text{kcal m}^{-2}\).
- As a rate of biomass production it is expressed as \(\text{g m}^{-2}\,\text{yr}^{-1}\) or \(\text{kcal m}^{-2}\,\text{yr}^{-1}\).

(b) Primary productivity depends on the plant species inhabiting an area, on environmental factors, on the availability of nutrients and on the photosynthetic capacity of the plants. A place in Rajasthan is low on all of these compared with one in Kerala (any three):
- fewer plant species and a sparse cover of vegetation;
- lower availability of nutrients in the dry, sandy soil;
- lower photosynthetic capacity of the plants, which are adapted to save water;
- an unfavourable environment: scarcity of water and high temperature.

Kerala, warm with heavy rainfall and dense vegetation, therefore produces far more organic matter per unit area per year.
